## Context

Ticket 0026 was ruled a monster on 2026-09-02 and decomposed by ladder rung;
this is rung 3 of five children, and 0026 is the tracker. Goal 3 is "it answers,
and it is honest about what it has" (README.md, "## The goals ladder"), and it
is the widest rung: seven terms, all seven this ticket's. R1 coverage
converging unattended, R4 the index answering while it is still filling, R6 the
query path waiting for no freshness work, R17 the coverage sentence, R18 which
emptiness an empty answer is, R19 at index time, and R32 the build-time bounds.
The instruments are the convergence harness and the soak, specified in SPEC.md
§5.2.8; every threshold and every bound is that section's, cited here and never
copied.

This is also the acceptance spec served to the harness offer, ticket 0032 — the
one artifact that survives reimplementation by design, offered upstream as tests
he can run against whatever he builds. So it is written as an executable spec
whose runnable-when condition is stated, not as a script that quietly does
nothing until the machinery exists.

0026's body said the harness "needs a mock Zotero local API (everything in
bench/ talks to live Zotero), which lands with them". It does not: tranche 1
(ticket 0551, closed) already built the record/replay fake and the synthetic
library, on the fork branch `conductor-integration`. That is a correction to the
tracker's premise and it removes a dependency this ticket would otherwise carry.
What it does wait on is ticket 0566, the assembled conductor: the harness drives
a real process from an empty data directory and polls status, and there is no
process to drive until 0566 lands.

## Relevant files

- `SPEC.md` §5.2.8, "**The convergence harness**" — the four assertions, and
  phases 2, 3 and 4. Phase 4 is the schema flip, which is R1's clause and not
  R23's: it asserts that coverage comes back, never that it was never lost.
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.8, "**R13, the soak gate**" — the soak's constants, the
  conductor-latency clause (a confirmation since 2026-09-02, not an acceptance
  gate) and the service clause beside it.
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.8, "**R32, the build-time gate**" — the reference machine, the
  rate, and why the bound is on the pipeline rather than on one stage.
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.8, "**Counters (C4)**" and "**The coverage sentence**" — what
  R17's assertions read.
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.9 — the census the wall-clock figures are derived through.
- Fork `tests/fixtures/local-api-replay.ts` and `synthetic-library.ts` — tranche
  1's record/replay fake of the Zotero local API and its ~50-item synthetic
  library. The fixture this harness points at; it exists.
- Fork branch `conductor-integration` — the integration base (ticket 0568,
  closed). `make upstream-checkout` recreates the git-ignored `fork/`.
- `tickets/0566-assemble-the-conductor-into-one-runnable.erg` — the assembled
  process this harness drives.
- `tickets/0032-the-harness-offer-acceptance-spec-as-the.erg` — the offer this
  spec is served to.

## Actions

1. The convergence harness: a fixture library, an empty data directory, status
   polls at 1 Hz touching nothing else, since R1 needs no asking. Assert the four
   things §5.2.8 states — status latency, coverage monotone, the class order per
   item, and the terminal state (all stages at total, drift 0, `pipeline: idle`,
   work counters stationary).
2. Phase 3, the hostile fixture: one quarantine, one 15k-page PDF, `dateAdded`
   ties. The harness MUST fail on the corpus that exercises its subtraction
   terms, not only pass on the gentle one.
3. Phase 4, the schema flip: restamp the built index to a foreign schema version
   in either direction, restart, and assert the terminal state returns
   unattended — nothing asked for, no file deleted by hand — inside R32's bounds.
   The distinguishing observable is the embed counters, flat where the index was
   migrated and not flat where it was abandoned.
4. R1's convergence-to-latest-chain SHOULD (0026's log, 2026-09-01T06:06Z):
   superseded work drains unattended after an extractor, segmenter or embedder
   upgrade, old results serving labeled until overtaken.
5. R4: queries run against the harness throughout the build and are answered.
   The state that makes this discriminating is a build in progress, which only
   this harness supplies.
6. R18: an empty answer says which emptiness it is. The discriminating state is
   the same partially built index — a scope that is genuinely not indexed yet.
7. R17 as four assertions, because its single MUST carries four clauses behind
   it: N of M, per stage, the most-recent-covered date, and metadata-only counted
   with its reason. The clause count is a floor and this one is written as four.
   The device clause — status names the execution device actually serving — is
   the disclosure gate's and gates on every machine.
8. R19 at index time: every token the query normalizer makes, the index
   normalizer can make too, asserted against a built index. The codepoint sweep
   that decides the same property without a build is ticket 0578's; this is the
   half that needs one.
9. R32, the build-time gate: assert the pipeline rate on every run and the wall
   clock whenever a full build is available, reporting each stage separately so a
   regression names its stage, and reporting the machine it ran on. Extract and
   chunk are reported and not asserted until ticket 0500 pins them; the gate
   fails only on the total.
10. The soak: assert what §5.2.8 lists, and record the conductor-latency clause
    as a confirmation rather than a gate. A failure there is more serious than it
    was, not less, since it is no longer explicable by the topology.
11. Every threshold and gate in the offered spec names the sqlite backend
    explicitly, per ticket 0032's own exit criteria.

## Test

The harness is itself a test, so what needs writing first is its own red state.
Two of them are structural and cheap. Phase 3's hostile fixture is one: build it
before phase 1's gentle corpus, because a harness that passes on the gentle
corpus and was never run on the hostile one has demonstrated nothing about its
subtraction terms. Phase 4's is the other: an index that was abandoned rather
than migrated must be distinguishable from one that was, and the embed counters
are the observable that separates them — run the abandoning arm and see the
counters move.

For R18, the red fixture is an empty answer that cannot say which emptiness it
is; assert against a scope that is not indexed yet and against one that is and
matched nothing, and require the two to differ.

## Verification

- [ ] The harness runs against ticket 0566's assembled process over tranche 1's
      fixtures and reaches the terminal state.
- [ ] Phase 3 ran and its hostile-corpus arm was seen to fail a harness missing
      its subtraction terms.
- [ ] Phase 4's two arms — migrated and abandoned — are told apart by the embed
      counters and not by elapsed time.
- [ ] R17 carries four assertions, each with its own id, not one.
- [ ] R32 reports per stage, asserts on the total, and names the machine.
- [ ] The soak's conductor-latency clause is recorded as a confirmation.
- [ ] Every threshold in the spec names the sqlite backend.
- [ ] `make check` green; `./tickets/erg check` green.

## Invariants

- The unit is the MUST clause, not the requirement: one assertion per clause, so
  README.md's `delivered` column becomes a count rather than a reader's
  judgement. Inherited from the tracker, ticket 0026. R17 is the worked example:
  one MUST, four assertions.
- Honest red and waiver states in the Makefile, never a green that means "could
  not look". Inherited from the tracker, ticket 0026. A harness that skips
  because the machinery is absent reports the same thing as one that is broken.
- No threshold, bound or budget is restated here. SPEC.md §5.2.8 owns the gates,
  §5.2.9 the budgets and the census.
- Phase 4 asserts R1's clause. Serving the foreign stamp without a rebuild is
  R23's own promise and ticket 0579's.
- The spec travels to ticket 0032 as a one-time transfer; this repository does
  not promise to track upstream's tree with it.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] R1 asserted: coverage converges unattended, newest-first, text-less
      attachment covered with its reason, the schema flip returning the terminal
      state, superseded work draining to the latest chain
- [ ] R4 asserted: queries answered throughout a build, its first included
- [ ] R6 asserted: the query path waits for no freshness work
- [ ] R17 asserted as four: N of M, per stage, most-recent-covered date,
      metadata-only counted with reason
- [ ] R18 asserted: the two emptinesses distinguishable, each against its own
      discriminating state
- [ ] R19 at index time asserted against a built index
- [ ] R32 asserted: the pipeline rate on every run, the wall clock on a full
      build, per-stage reporting, the machine named
- [ ] the soak's assertions written, the conductor-latency clause recorded as a
      confirmation and the service clause beside it
- [ ] the whole is runnable against ticket 0566's assembled process and is the
      spec ticket 0032 offers
